## The three problems

**`parser`: the shared parser reads every date month first.** Every tenant on file writes the
day first, so 7 April comes out as 4 July. Day and month swap places; the body names both dates.

**`hint`: booking-v3's form tells people to type month first.** Its date field prints
`MM/DD/YYYY`, and the form then reads the date the tenant's way, day first. People who follow the
hint get the same swap, but the parser fix does not touch it: the hint and the form disagree.
Only reports sent through the form of a booking-v3 tenant (Alder Physio, Copper Studio, Pale
Harbour) have this cause.

**`one-day`: the date moves by one day, it does not swap.** "Tuesday 12 May. Calendar says
Monday 11 May." "Client in Lisbon got Tuesday. Client in Toronto got Monday." "The invite arrived
before I made the booking." A flipped day and month cannot do that; a time zone or a day boundary
can. The desk already read these as a one-day move (`desk-notes.json`), then filed them under
`parser` by tenant.

<Callout kind="warn">
The fix queued for #4471 closes <code>parser</code> only. <code>hint</code> and <code>one-day</code>
stay open until they have their own fix.
</Callout>

## Why one ticket held all three

[Decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) merges on subject alone. All three
problems arrive as "Wrong day on the invite" or "Month and day swapped?", so they all landed in one
ticket. The intake tag does not separate them either: it is guessed from the reporter's browser,
not their tenant, and it often disagrees with the cause. The table above counts by tenant, form and
body, and counts each report id once.

